<%# THE PURPOSE LEDGER'S BAND (two-ledger spec §2): what the money is FOR.
    Local: `presenter` (HomePresenter).

    ONE BAND, NOT ONE PER ACCOUNT. The pool era drew a section per account and hung that account's
    envelopes inside it, because an envelope physically lived in a bank account and could only be
    funded from it. A category lives nowhere — allocating money is an act of intention rather than
    of location (§2) — so there is one list, in the one order a distribution fills it, and the
    accounts band below says nothing about it at all.

    THE HEADER IS AVAILABLE, which is the root the rows are funded out of and the exact analogue of
    the account header's `buffer now` in the old shape: money with no job yet (§7.1's buffer,
    re-anchored). It is `HomePresenter#available` — post-sweep, the same figure the distribute
    screen would hand out — so the number here and the number the button below acts on are one. %>
<section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded" data-category-band>
  <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-200 flex justify-between items-baseline gap-4">
    <%# Not the `uppercase` eyebrow this codebase gives short labels: this is the title of a band,
        and it takes the same weight as the attention band's heading and the account headers. %>
    <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Categories</h3>
    <%# "available now", for the reason the account header says "balance now": two money figures
        from two different moments sharing a bare noun is a defect this screen has already shipped
        once. The standing band above states what is left AFTER this period's distribution; this is
        the figure that distribution starts from.

        ** IT IS POST-SWEEP, AND THAT IS ONE FIGURE ON THIS SCREEN AND NOT TWO. ** It is
        `HomePresenter#available` — `CategoryLedger#available` PLUS what the next distribution
        sweeps back out of closed categories — which is `AllocationCalculator#available` to the
        character, so the number here and the number the Distribute button hands out are the same
        one. That is the whole reason the sweep is in it.

        THE FIX BUTTONS IN THE ATTENTION BAND ARE CEILINGED ON A DIFFERENT QUANTITY, deliberately:
        `#free_amount_for(ROOT)` reads the PLAIN `CategoryLedger#available`, because a hand move
        happens NOW, out of money that is genuinely unclaimed today, and `Allocation
        #source_must_hold_it` would refuse a move funded out of a sweep nobody has written. So a
        closed category's leftover is inside this header and outside that ceiling, and the two are
        about different acts rather than about different opinions of one act. Neither figure is
        labelled the other's word: this one says "now", the button says what it would move. %>
    <span class="text-sm <%= presenter.available.negative? ? "text-status-danger font-medium" : "text-gray-700" %>">
      available now <strong><%= number_to_currency(presenter.available) %></strong>
    </span>
  </div>

  <% presenter.categories.each do |category| %>
    <%= render "holding_row", category: category, presenter: presenter %>
  <% end %>

  <%# Said plainly rather than left as an empty card: a user whose categories hold nothing yet is
      in a setup state, and a blank panel reads as something that failed to load. The door is the
      Budget page, which is where a rule — the thing that starts a category holding money — is
      written. %>
  <% if presenter.categories.empty? %>
    <p class="px-6 py-3 text-sm text-gray-500">
      No category holds money yet — every expense comes out of what's available.
      <%= link_to "Give one a rule on the Budget page", budget_page_path, class: "text-status-info hover:underline" %>
    </p>
  <% end %>
</section>
